			"22": {
				"attributes": {
					"alignment": {
						"type": "integer",
						"value": 0
					},
					"fill": {
						"type": "bool",
						"value": false
					},
					"font-size": {
						"type": "float",
						"value": 19
					},
					"text": {
						"type": "string",
						"value": "COLLECTIONS\nA signal that contains instances is referred to as a collection.\nYou can recognize a connection that holds a collection by its striped cord.\n\nIn the example below the Linear node sends out a collection of Float2's and the Circle sends out a single Shape2D.\nNote how the instance count of the signal gets updated to the instance count of the Linear node.\nThe collection size of the Linear node dictates the size of the collection.\n\nThis is important to understand because Wire will always update to the largest instance count.\nLater in the example we use the Join node to create a collection of 2 colors.\nNote how the colors are repeated over the instances.\n\nInstead of reducing the signal to 2 shapes, the colors are repeated to the largest collection.\n\nAssignment: Play around with the size of the Linear node.\n"
					}
				},
